 <main class="wrap">
 <p class="kicker">Essay 2 of 6 · Border files for CEE imports · from pitch 7</p>
 <h1>The border creates the file. The broker owns the border.</h1>
-<p class="lede">A Dutch steel importer can clear its next load only if the right declarant sits behind the customs entry. Its later carbon bill depends on data held by the foreign mill. That gap appears made for a regulatory reader who can call the supplier in its own language. The fatal point is control. The importer or customs representative keeps the legal role, while a broker can bundle the same data chase into clearance. The first purchase is therefore not a grand compliance retainer. It is one paid supplier-evidence diagnostic, sold through a Dutch declarant, which proves that language can recover better data than the default-value route.</p>
+<p class="lede">A Dutch steel importer above the threshold needs an authorised declarant at the border. This entrant cannot supply that status or release a stopped load. Its narrower chance comes before the annual declaration, when finance must decide whether verified mill data saves more certificate cash than Commission defaults. That gap suits a regulatory reader who can call the supplier in its own language. The fatal point is control. The importer or customs representative keeps the legal role, while a broker can bundle the same data chase into clearance. The first purchase is one paid supplier-evidence diagnostic, sold through a Dutch declarant.</p>
 
 <section class="proposition">
 <h2>The proposition</h2>
-<p>The first buyer is the finance, procurement or customs lead of a Dutch metal importer above the CBAM threshold. It buys steel or aluminium from one Ukrainian, Turkish or Kazakh installation. The purchase event is a stopped import, an authorisation review, or the decision between default and actual emissions before the first annual declaration.</p>
+<p>The first buyer is the finance, procurement or customs lead of a Dutch metal importer above the CBAM threshold. It buys steel or aluminium from one Ukrainian, Turkish or Kazakh installation. The primary purchase event is its actual-versus-default decision before the first annual declaration. A stopped import instead triggers the broker's separate authorisation service and may create a later referral.</p>
 <p>The initial unit is one importer, one producing installation and one customs-code family. The fixed product is a <em>supplier-evidence diagnostic</em>. It reconciles customs quantities, identifies the declared production route, obtains the supplier's available monitoring record, maps missing evidence, and compares the two lawful reporting routes. It ends with a handover pack for the importer or its authorised customs representative.</p>
 <p>The business does not clear goods, become importer of record, buy certificates, verify emissions or give legal advice. A verifier must verify actual emissions. <!-- CHECK: 02-actual-verification --> The authorised declarant remains responsible for the declaration. <!-- CHECK: 02-declarant-responsibility --> The entrant coordinates the foreign installation and prepares evidence for those accountable parties. It never becomes software with a login.</p>
 <p>The substitute is strong: use Commission default values, let procurement email the supplier, and have the existing customs broker submit the file. Default values require no verified installation report, although the Dutch authority says they usually create higher CBAM costs. <!-- CHECK: 02-default-route --> The service wins only where the avoidable certificate cost and internal effort exceed its fee.</p>
@@ -38,12 +38,12 @@
 <p>CBAM is no longer a coming rule. Its definitive regime began on 1 January 2026. <!-- CHECK: 02-definitive-start --> The Dutch threshold is more than 50 tonnes per importer per calendar year across covered goods, excluding electricity and hydrogen. Once crossed, the obligations apply to all covered imports in that year. <!-- CHECK: 02-cbam-threshold --> The covered physical categories include iron and steel, aluminium, cement, fertilisers, electricity and hydrogen. <!-- CHECK: 02-cbam-sectors --></p>
 <p>An EU importer above the threshold needs authorised-declarant status, unless an indirect customs representative agrees to take over the CBAM obligations. Customs checks the status at import and does not release the goods without it. <!-- CHECK: 02-cbam-authorisation --> Applications filed by 31 March 2026 received a temporary pending-decision route. <!-- CHECK: 02-cbam-grace --> That date has passed. A later applicant that crosses the threshold must wait for approval, for which Customs states a maximum 120-day treatment period. <!-- CHECK: 02-cbam-late-application --></p>
 <p>The recurring event sits later. The first annual declaration covers 2026 and is due by 30 September 2027. <!-- CHECK: 02-cbam-declaration-date --> The obligation exists now, while much of the cash and filing work arrives next year. Enforcement creates urgency for authorisation. It does not prove a 2026 consulting budget for supplier data.</p>
-<p>The actual-data route is narrower than the pitch implied. A producing installation prepares an emissions report, and an accredited CBAM verifier checks it. The entrant can organise inputs but cannot turn unverified mill figures into actual values. In July 2026 the NEa said no accredited CBAM verifiers were yet available; nine firms had applied to the Dutch accreditation council and about thirty more had shown interest. <!-- CHECK: 02-verifier-capacity --> Default values therefore remain both a substitute and a bridge.</p>
+<p>The actual-data route is narrower than the pitch implied. A producing installation prepares an emissions report, and an accredited CBAM verifier checks it. <!-- CHECK: 02-actual-verification --> The entrant can organise inputs but cannot turn unverified mill figures into actual values. In July 2026 the NEa said no accredited CBAM verifiers were yet available; nine firms had applied to the Dutch accreditation council and about thirty more had shown interest. <!-- CHECK: 02-verifier-capacity --> Default values therefore remain both a substitute and a bridge.</p>
 <p>The rule nevertheless puts a price on better evidence. Standard values require no verified installation report. They receive an uplift of 10% for 2026, 20% for 2027 and 30% from 2028; the NEa says they usually produce higher costs than actual data. <!-- CHECK: 02-default-route --> That difference is the budget mechanism. It still must be calculated for one importer's codes, origin, quantity and production route before anyone quotes a service.</p>
 <p>The pitch's number cannot be settled as asked. A March 2026 NEa newsletter reported almost 1,100 applications, 870 approved, and about 250 new applications after the definitive period began. It also said increasing numbers of applicants were below 50 tonnes and needed no authorisation. <!-- CHECK: 02-application-count --> An earlier NEa implementation estimate put the post-threshold Dutch population at no more than 1,500 declarants, based on 2023–2025 import statistics. <!-- CHECK: 02-population-estimate --></p>
 <p>Those records do not identify how many applicants were above 50 tonnes, how many filed before 31 March, or how many eligible importers missed the date. <!-- CHECK: 02-count-boundary --> The requested numerator and denominator are not public. The honest addressable record is “no more than 1,500” plus an application count contaminated by unnecessary filings. That is enough to reject market-size theatre, not enough to forecast customers.</p>
-<p>EUDR moved the other way. An enacted December 2025 amendment delayed general application to 30 December 2026 and most micro or small operators to 30 June 2027. It also reduced repeated downstream submissions by concentrating the due-diligence statement on the first operator placing the product on the EU market. <!-- CHECK: 02-eudr-revision --> The deadline has slipped twice, so budget timing deserves skepticism.</p>
-<p>Ukraine is classified low-risk. Low-risk sourcing permits simplified due diligence when origin is unmixed, though the operator must still collect compliance information. <!-- CHECK: 02-ukraine-risk --> <!-- CHECK: 02-low-risk-effect --> More damaging to the original pitch, Ukraine's State Forest Agency says its tracking system already follows batches from harvest to export and provides legality documents and geolocation tools. <!-- CHECK: 02-ukraine-system --> The file may still need assembly in the EU system. The claim that polygons must be extracted by phone is no longer credible.</p>
+<p>EUDR moved the other way. Regulation (EU) 2025/2650 delayed general application to 30 December 2026 and most qualifying micro or small operators to 30 June 2027. It replaced repeated downstream statement submissions with information-collection and retention duties. <!-- CHECK: 02-eudr-revision --> That timing weakens the case for a near-term wood budget.</p>
+<p>Ukraine is classified low-risk. <!-- CHECK: 02-ukraine-risk --> Qualifying low-risk sourcing permits simplified due diligence, though the operator must still collect compliance information. <!-- CHECK: 02-low-risk-effect --> More damaging to the original pitch, a State Forest Agency page posted 15 April 2026 presents batch-tracking and legality services in response to questions about proving harvest-site geolocation. <!-- CHECK: 02-ukraine-system --> The file may still need assembly in the EU system. The claim that polygons must be extracted by phone is no longer credible.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="whyyou">
@@ -52,7 +52,7 @@
 <p>Regulatory literacy supplies the boundary. It separates threshold, authorisation, actual values, default values, verification and certificate cash. The engineering habit turns an email chase into a data lineage: quantity to customs entry, good to code, product to installation, installation to process, process to source record. Internal automation can reduce errors, but the customer buys a signed handover pack, not access to a tool.</p>
 <p>The Dutch-language gate is conditional, not passed. No Dutch trade exam appears in the CBAM roles reviewed here; the legal roles are importer, authorised declarant, indirect representative and accredited verifier. <!-- CHECK: 02-credential-boundary --> The operator's technical emissions report must be submitted in English under the implementing regulation. <!-- CHECK: 02-english-report --> That helps with the mill. It does not make A2 sufficient for Dutch cold sales, customs disputes or a finance director's risk questions.</p>
 <p>The answer is a Dutch customs partner. The partner owns the local relationship, customs representation and Dutch explanation. The entrant contracts as its named CEE supplier-data specialist. Customs confirms that a service party may deliver the CBAM filing on the importer's behalf through chain authorisation, while the importer remains liable. <!-- CHECK: 02-service-provider-role --> If the partner insists on owning the method, supplier relationship and all margin, the channel fails.</p>
